WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:01.250
All right.

00:00:01.250 --> 00:00:02.520
Good evening, everybody.

00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:03.920
My name is Shannon
Rose McAuliff.

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:05.630
I'm joined here by Theo Fields.

00:00:05.630 --> 00:00:07.020
Say hello, Theo.

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:07.520
Hello.

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:11.690
We are here to present the fifth
workshop in the Creative Arts

00:00:11.690 --> 00:00:12.950
Competition Series.

00:00:12.950 --> 00:00:15.860
This evening we have
Miguel Rodriguez with us,

00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:17.750
and he's going to
be taking us through

00:00:17.750 --> 00:00:21.860
these fundamental pillars of
a non-profit business plan.

00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:24.800
So we'll do a bit
of presentation

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:28.040
and then we will open
the thing for Q&amp;A.

00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:30.800
We'll have some really
lively discussion.

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:34.160
And with that, I will just
turn it over to Miguel

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:36.080
to give a quick
self-introduction

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:38.360
and then get us underway.

00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:41.390
Thank you so much, Shannon
Rose, and thank you

00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:45.500
for inviting me to be part
of this series of workshops

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:47.210
that you are offering.

00:00:47.210 --> 00:00:50.960
I am the founder and
president of Athlone Artists,

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:54.960
which is an artist
representation agency.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:58.940
It's a boutique agency
representing about 51 artists

00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:01.100
so far, internationally.

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:04.459
These are classical musicians,
opera singers, conductors,

00:01:04.459 --> 00:01:06.380
and stage directors.

00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:12.470
I spent the past 25 years
working in the arts management

00:01:12.470 --> 00:01:19.820
sector managing some of
Massachusetts most interesting

00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:21.980
organizations, such as
Boston Baroque, where

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:25.340
I spent my last 8 years
before I founded my agency,

00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:29.570
and Opera Boston, the Boston
Landmarks Orchestra, the Fuller

00:01:29.570 --> 00:01:34.050
Craft Museum and a few more.

00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:35.960
So what I figure--

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:37.760
I'm going to share
my screen because I'm

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:40.670
going to do a little bit of
a PowerPoint presentation.

00:01:40.670 --> 00:01:44.240
I'm going to go through
it rather quickly.

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:46.100
If you have any
questions, please

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:50.060
do unmute yourself and say,
"Miguel, I have a question,"

00:01:50.060 --> 00:01:52.190
and pop in and ask.

00:01:52.190 --> 00:01:55.680
It's the easiest
way in this format.

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:59.120
And then we will
also have a chance

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:00.990
to have a little bit
more of a conversation.

00:02:00.990 --> 00:02:01.907
So I'm going to share.

00:02:04.650 --> 00:02:08.580
OK, here we are.

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:09.479
This here.

00:02:09.479 --> 00:02:14.670
OK, today I thought that we not
talk about mission and vision.

00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:16.470
Because I'm sure
a lot you've been

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:18.840
talking about this in
the past, and you're

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:20.160
very familiar with that.

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:28.140
But I thought I'd speak about
the long term and short terms

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:30.810
of planning for the
non-profit-- going

00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:35.820
beyond the mission and vision.

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:37.920
The overview of the talk is--

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:40.360
number one, I'm going to talk
about building your team.

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:42.450
What does that mean?

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:46.080
What has been my
experience on criteria

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:48.840
for building the right
team for my institution

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:51.690
for my organization
for my mission?

00:02:51.690 --> 00:02:54.630
Number two-- I'm going
to talk of building

00:02:54.630 --> 00:02:56.820
your network of stakeholders.

00:02:56.820 --> 00:02:59.410
Those are the people that
represent your market.

00:02:59.410 --> 00:03:02.880
Who are you bringing your
services, or your product,

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:04.560
or your programs to?

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:07.485
And number three, I'm going
to talk about the sordid topic

00:03:07.485 --> 00:03:09.606
of coin--

00:03:09.606 --> 00:03:14.940
what we call the fundraising
part of the non-profit sector.

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:17.130
And basically, how
do we capitalize?

00:03:17.130 --> 00:03:25.650
How do we infuse and inject
capital into our organization?

00:03:25.650 --> 00:03:28.770
And there are two strategies
that I'm going to talk about.

00:03:28.770 --> 00:03:30.390
First of all, fundraising.

00:03:30.390 --> 00:03:35.080
And then I'm going
to talk about--

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:36.780
I'm so sorry.

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:42.990
I'm going to turn this off so
that we are not interrupted.

00:03:42.990 --> 00:03:45.540
And then I'm going to talk
about the two strategies--

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:47.160
fundraising, which
is short term,

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:50.020
and then capitalization,
which is the longer term.

00:03:50.020 --> 00:03:51.570
Again, this is a conversation.

00:03:51.570 --> 00:03:53.620
Please ask questions.

00:03:53.620 --> 00:03:55.270
There are many
ways to skin a cat

00:03:55.270 --> 00:03:59.230
and I'm just going to touch
the surface of some of them.

00:03:59.230 --> 00:04:03.460
First of all, talking
about building your team.

00:04:03.460 --> 00:04:06.450
This is really important--
probably the most important

00:04:06.450 --> 00:04:09.190
part of the startup process.

00:04:09.190 --> 00:04:10.620
First of all,
choosing the people

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:12.450
with the right expertise.

00:04:12.450 --> 00:04:15.250
What are the areas that
you need help with?

00:04:15.250 --> 00:04:17.670
What are the things that
you need to accomplish?

00:04:17.670 --> 00:04:20.820
And how do you get the
right people on board?

00:04:20.820 --> 00:04:25.080
Over the years, I have been a
firm believer of Good to Great.

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:28.350
It was a book written
by Jim Collins,

00:04:28.350 --> 00:04:32.350
and it helps you to think
about your institution,

00:04:32.350 --> 00:04:34.350
your business, your mission--

00:04:34.350 --> 00:04:36.420
whatever you're trying
to build as a bus,

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:40.485
and you need to get on the bus
the right number of people.

00:04:43.240 --> 00:04:45.960
One of the things
that he talks about is

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:49.110
to retain the right
people before embarking

00:04:49.110 --> 00:04:52.030
on any specific program is key.

00:04:52.030 --> 00:04:56.160
You can't get started if you
don't have a bus running.

00:04:56.160 --> 00:04:59.070
I always say, don't
look for compliments.

00:04:59.070 --> 00:05:02.040
Look for people that complement
you-- not that are equals.

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:04.150
And what do I mean by that?

00:05:04.150 --> 00:05:07.650
It's very easy, and I think
it's the most common mistake

00:05:07.650 --> 00:05:10.800
for people to hire people that
have the same affinity of you--

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:12.780
that are like you.

00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:16.680
It's easy to get along with
people that you feel have

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:21.900
the same type of mentality.

00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:25.100
And, oh, so sorry about this.

00:05:25.100 --> 00:05:26.900
Let me just quit this.

00:05:26.900 --> 00:05:30.160
There we go.

00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:33.870
So getting people who are
not just like you but who

00:05:33.870 --> 00:05:35.040
compliment you.

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:36.210
What are the things?

00:05:36.210 --> 00:05:39.930
What are the skill sets
that you don't have that you

00:05:39.930 --> 00:05:41.520
need to bring on board?

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:45.060
I want to bring people always
into my organization who

00:05:45.060 --> 00:05:48.480
complement what I don't have.

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:50.760
If my strengths
are programmatic,

00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:53.190
and my strengths
are not analytical,

00:05:53.190 --> 00:05:56.010
and they are not
marketing, then I

00:05:56.010 --> 00:06:00.990
want people who are very
strong in those areas--

00:06:00.990 --> 00:06:06.870
not necessarily people
who are similar to me.

00:06:06.870 --> 00:06:12.870
The other thing--
your team management

00:06:12.870 --> 00:06:14.730
should include people
who care very deeply

00:06:14.730 --> 00:06:17.220
about what your mission
is, about your company,

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:20.250
and who will argue
passionately for the decisions

00:06:20.250 --> 00:06:23.970
that they believe are right,
even if they disagree with you.

00:06:23.970 --> 00:06:27.090
Another part that is
very important for me

00:06:27.090 --> 00:06:32.520
is to make an assessment and
know the leadership styles

00:06:32.520 --> 00:06:34.400
within my management team.

00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:37.230
What do I mean by that?

00:06:37.230 --> 00:06:40.980
Over the years, there have been
many ways to assess styles--

00:06:40.980 --> 00:06:42.120
leadership styles.

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:46.120
And you want to make sure
that you have an array--

00:06:46.120 --> 00:06:50.800
a variety of styles within
your management team.

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:54.090
The most four common, and
these have different names

00:06:54.090 --> 00:06:58.920
by different theorists,
are directors, analyzers,

00:06:58.920 --> 00:07:00.900
expressers, and harmonizers.

00:07:00.900 --> 00:07:04.510
And I'm going to talk
about those in a moment.

00:07:04.510 --> 00:07:08.880
And then the last thing
that I encourage everyone

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:11.370
to think about when
you are thinking

00:07:11.370 --> 00:07:15.290
about starting an institution--

00:07:15.290 --> 00:07:17.520
an organization of any kind--

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:21.640
is to think about how
does equity, diversity,

00:07:21.640 --> 00:07:25.740
and inclusion become
part of your frameworks

00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:27.130
from the beginning?

00:07:27.130 --> 00:07:31.350
How do you create an environment
of equity within your team

00:07:31.350 --> 00:07:33.240
and within your company?

00:07:33.240 --> 00:07:38.790
And that includes your
stakeholders and your market.

00:07:38.790 --> 00:07:43.110
And second, how do you diversify
in all ways of what it means?

00:07:43.110 --> 00:07:47.610
Diversity-- not just racial
but age groups, skill sets.

00:07:47.610 --> 00:07:48.430
All of that.

00:07:48.430 --> 00:07:51.030
How do you create a
diverse environment?

00:07:51.030 --> 00:07:55.560
And how do you become a culture
of inclusion within your team?

00:07:55.560 --> 00:07:56.730
And you build that.

00:07:56.730 --> 00:08:02.070
And that is something that has
become very important today.

00:08:02.070 --> 00:08:07.500
And funders in corporations
and foundations--

00:08:07.500 --> 00:08:09.330
they are all very
interested in learning

00:08:09.330 --> 00:08:12.810
about the culture of equity,
diversity, and inclusion

00:08:12.810 --> 00:08:15.040
within your organization.

00:08:15.040 --> 00:08:18.090
So let me tell you about
what leadership styles and

00:08:18.090 --> 00:08:20.680
what I mean by that.

00:08:20.680 --> 00:08:22.830
There are four
leadership styles,

00:08:22.830 --> 00:08:27.270
and when we talk about these,
we don't compartmentalize people

00:08:27.270 --> 00:08:28.650
within one of those.

00:08:28.650 --> 00:08:33.100
There are people that move
between two categories.

00:08:33.100 --> 00:08:35.100
But let me just tell you
a little bit about them

00:08:35.100 --> 00:08:38.520
and why they are important,
and why it's important for you

00:08:38.520 --> 00:08:42.780
to know the leadership
styles of your team.

00:08:42.780 --> 00:08:44.620
First of all, we
have "directors."

00:08:44.620 --> 00:08:46.980
These are people who
are goal-oriented.

00:08:46.980 --> 00:08:49.080
They are quick at
making decisions.

00:08:49.080 --> 00:08:50.550
They are always on the go.

00:08:50.550 --> 00:08:52.290
They tell it as it is.

00:08:52.290 --> 00:08:54.060
If you give them
two choices, they'll

00:08:54.060 --> 00:08:56.580
make a decision really fast.

00:08:56.580 --> 00:09:00.330
These people get a
lot done very quickly.

00:09:00.330 --> 00:09:04.920
Sometimes they can be perceived
as insensitive because they

00:09:04.920 --> 00:09:07.500
don't dwell on the issue--

00:09:07.500 --> 00:09:08.550
they create.

00:09:08.550 --> 00:09:09.840
They look at the solution.

00:09:09.840 --> 00:09:12.810
They make their
decision and they go.

00:09:12.810 --> 00:09:19.350
These are very key individuals
needed in any organizations.

00:09:19.350 --> 00:09:21.990
And CEOs-- a lot of CEOs--

00:09:21.990 --> 00:09:27.690
and a lot of executive directors
fall under this category.

00:09:27.690 --> 00:09:29.610
Number 2 are the "analyzers."

00:09:29.610 --> 00:09:34.260
And I always think of analyzers
as the 180 degree opposite

00:09:34.260 --> 00:09:35.740
from the directors.

00:09:35.740 --> 00:09:39.130
These are people who
are very data-driven.

00:09:39.130 --> 00:09:40.770
They are task-oriented.

00:09:40.770 --> 00:09:43.110
They deal with process.

00:09:43.110 --> 00:09:45.150
They know how to process--

00:09:45.150 --> 00:09:48.450
how to create process
to get a product out.

00:09:48.450 --> 00:09:51.300
They make lists.

00:09:51.300 --> 00:09:53.550
They are called, also, thinkers.

00:09:53.550 --> 00:09:58.410
These are people that
will review data over

00:09:58.410 --> 00:10:03.240
and over and over, and they will
have a conversation with you

00:10:03.240 --> 00:10:07.110
for 20 minutes about
one particular thing.

00:10:07.110 --> 00:10:10.530
Analyzers can drive
directors crazy

00:10:10.530 --> 00:10:12.780
because directors are
like, make a decision,

00:10:12.780 --> 00:10:17.520
and the analyzer wants to just
go over the data over and over

00:10:17.520 --> 00:10:18.660
again.

00:10:18.660 --> 00:10:21.600
They are sometimes
perceived as procrastinators

00:10:21.600 --> 00:10:25.450
because they take a long
time to make a decision.

00:10:25.450 --> 00:10:29.100
However, directors
need analyzers.

00:10:29.100 --> 00:10:32.730
They give directors
the right choices--

00:10:32.730 --> 00:10:38.400
the right choices
to make a decision.

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:41.850
They are the people who
think the hardest about how

00:10:41.850 --> 00:10:46.050
to solve a problem and what are
the repercussions going to be.

00:10:46.050 --> 00:10:48.780
The directors will
look at that quickly

00:10:48.780 --> 00:10:51.960
and make a decision based on
what the analyzers give them.

00:10:51.960 --> 00:10:54.810
But these folks take
a little bit longer,

00:10:54.810 --> 00:10:57.030
and you need to be able
to identify them and work

00:10:57.030 --> 00:10:57.780
with them.

00:10:57.780 --> 00:10:59.610
Then we can have
the "expressers."

00:10:59.610 --> 00:11:02.970
These are, especially
in social services,

00:11:02.970 --> 00:11:05.470
these are people-oriented
individuals.

00:11:05.470 --> 00:11:06.540
They are animated.

00:11:06.540 --> 00:11:07.530
They are entertaining.

00:11:07.530 --> 00:11:09.450
They are the life of a crowd.

00:11:09.450 --> 00:11:13.260
They are extroverts
most of the time.

00:11:13.260 --> 00:11:17.280
They may be imprecise because
position and data is not

00:11:17.280 --> 00:11:18.250
their focus.

00:11:18.250 --> 00:11:22.710
They are more focused on
learning about the person

00:11:22.710 --> 00:11:26.250
and learning about a
particular individual

00:11:26.250 --> 00:11:29.580
and being attuned with them.

00:11:29.580 --> 00:11:32.850
And they are a lot
of the times confused

00:11:32.850 --> 00:11:35.950
with the "harmonizers," which
are the relationship builders.

00:11:35.950 --> 00:11:37.980
These are the folks
that really, really

00:11:37.980 --> 00:11:40.950
want to get people engaged.

00:11:40.950 --> 00:11:42.420
And they are talkers.

00:11:42.420 --> 00:11:45.750
They talk very
calmly and precise,

00:11:45.750 --> 00:11:48.870
and they try to bring
people into the fold.

00:11:48.870 --> 00:11:50.190
They are very loyal.

00:11:50.190 --> 00:11:52.080
They are very dedicated.

00:11:52.080 --> 00:11:54.240
They are conflict-avert.

00:11:54.240 --> 00:11:56.040
They don't want to argue.

00:11:56.040 --> 00:11:58.290
They want to figure
out how can we solve

00:11:58.290 --> 00:12:01.080
the problem in an amicable way.

00:12:01.080 --> 00:12:02.610
They may not over-commit.

00:12:02.610 --> 00:12:06.220
They will not commit
at first glance.

00:12:06.220 --> 00:12:10.590
They will want to see how
things are going to go.

00:12:10.590 --> 00:12:14.100
These four styles live
in every organization

00:12:14.100 --> 00:12:16.170
and hopefully they do.

00:12:16.170 --> 00:12:19.380
If you have an organization
where all your teams are

00:12:19.380 --> 00:12:23.760
directors, there is going
to be a lot of mistakes

00:12:23.760 --> 00:12:28.680
made because there is
lack of data analysis.

00:12:28.680 --> 00:12:33.300
They are going to not
relate well to their market,

00:12:33.300 --> 00:12:36.400
to their stakeholders,
to their board members.

00:12:36.400 --> 00:12:39.330
They are not interested
in relationship building.

00:12:39.330 --> 00:12:43.980
They are short-lived and
one-term relationship.

00:12:43.980 --> 00:12:47.380
They are too quick.

00:12:47.380 --> 00:12:51.960
And so it is really important,
when you're building your team,

00:12:51.960 --> 00:12:54.180
that you bring on
board people that

00:12:54.180 --> 00:12:58.030
fill these categories-- that
fall into these categories

00:12:58.030 --> 00:13:02.190
so that you have a very
diverse number of thinkers

00:13:02.190 --> 00:13:06.540
and workers in your
team that can analyze,

00:13:06.540 --> 00:13:10.200
that can build
relationships with folks,

00:13:10.200 --> 00:13:12.540
can make decisions quickly.

00:13:12.540 --> 00:13:14.880
And that is going
to give you an edge.

00:13:14.880 --> 00:13:17.640
These are the people that
you want on your bus.

00:13:20.760 --> 00:13:23.850
Let's see.

00:13:23.850 --> 00:13:26.400
Building your network
of stakeholders.

00:13:32.810 --> 00:13:35.610
This is very important.

00:13:35.610 --> 00:13:38.780
This is how you
build the folks that

00:13:38.780 --> 00:13:42.230
are going to be supporting
your organization.

00:13:42.230 --> 00:13:46.130
They are going to be
folks that are going

00:13:46.130 --> 00:13:50.120
to bring capital and
streamline sustainability

00:13:50.120 --> 00:13:53.090
for short and long term.

00:13:53.090 --> 00:13:55.700
Always remember that
everybody that you speak

00:13:55.700 --> 00:13:59.090
to is a possible stakeholder.

00:13:59.090 --> 00:14:03.920
These are people that share
your purpose and passion.

00:14:03.920 --> 00:14:05.990
You should always
have a strategy

00:14:05.990 --> 00:14:09.140
for first-time meetings
with prospects.

00:14:09.140 --> 00:14:12.080
If you are going to meet with
someone for the first time

00:14:12.080 --> 00:14:14.480
or you're going to
have a cold call,

00:14:14.480 --> 00:14:16.670
have a strategy of
how you are going

00:14:16.670 --> 00:14:20.630
to get this person
interested in your mission.

00:14:20.630 --> 00:14:23.660
You've all heard about
the 60 second of the 30

00:14:23.660 --> 00:14:26.420
second elevator speech.

00:14:26.420 --> 00:14:30.350
There are many things--
practice those things.

00:14:30.350 --> 00:14:34.190
You need to identify-- what
are the things that connect you

00:14:34.190 --> 00:14:37.778
with those individuals?

00:14:37.778 --> 00:14:39.260
What do you share?

00:14:39.260 --> 00:14:42.710
I am always
interested in learning

00:14:42.710 --> 00:14:44.660
what ticks that person.

00:14:44.660 --> 00:14:48.080
For example, if I was
working at Boston Baroque

00:14:48.080 --> 00:14:50.480
and I was meeting a
donor-- a possible donor--

00:14:50.480 --> 00:14:53.750
for the first time, I'm
interested in what his history

00:14:53.750 --> 00:14:56.060
is with Baroque music.

00:14:56.060 --> 00:14:57.740
Because most
probably, this person

00:14:57.740 --> 00:15:01.390
is a business person that
enjoys the music genre.

00:15:01.390 --> 00:15:05.030
So how did they came
about to the organization

00:15:05.030 --> 00:15:06.138
or to this music genre?

00:15:06.138 --> 00:15:07.430
And what is it that ticks them?

00:15:07.430 --> 00:15:10.340
I'm always curious about that.

00:15:10.340 --> 00:15:13.520
Have a strategy for how
you're going to do this.

00:15:13.520 --> 00:15:16.820
You should identify people
within your network--

00:15:16.820 --> 00:15:19.820
that can be board members,
staffs, and stakeholders.

00:15:19.820 --> 00:15:23.780
And learn how to use your board,
your staff, and stakeholders

00:15:23.780 --> 00:15:27.110
as connectors to other
prospects and individuals.

00:15:27.110 --> 00:15:29.300
This is a machine
that is ongoing.

00:15:29.300 --> 00:15:32.570
It's a cycle that lives
in every organization.

00:15:32.570 --> 00:15:35.480
And every CEO and
executive director

00:15:35.480 --> 00:15:45.440
lives in this 24-hour, 365 cycle
of generating stakeholders.

00:15:45.440 --> 00:15:48.960
Always try to connect with
each person in some way--

00:15:48.960 --> 00:15:52.430
especially very important
stakeholders, important donors,

00:15:52.430 --> 00:15:56.060
and possible investors.

00:15:56.060 --> 00:15:58.910
I used to have a
little trick that I

00:15:58.910 --> 00:16:04.370
used to play when I had
meetings with prospective donors

00:16:04.370 --> 00:16:05.610
and I didn't know them.

00:16:05.610 --> 00:16:06.660
It was my first time.

00:16:06.660 --> 00:16:12.110
So I had a little folder that
I carry with me and a pad

00:16:12.110 --> 00:16:13.160
where I would take notes.

00:16:13.160 --> 00:16:18.290
And inside of it, on one
side, I had three pictures.

00:16:18.290 --> 00:16:20.180
This is when I was
working at Opera Boston.

00:16:20.180 --> 00:16:23.600
I had a picture of my dog.

00:16:23.600 --> 00:16:26.960
I had a picture of my niece--

00:16:26.960 --> 00:16:29.210
my young niece was 3 years old.

00:16:29.210 --> 00:16:31.850
And I had a picture
of Beverly Sills--

00:16:31.850 --> 00:16:33.470
a postcard of Beverly Sills.

00:16:33.470 --> 00:16:35.930
And I would open it and
they would look at it

00:16:35.930 --> 00:16:38.060
and they would go,
oh, is that your dog?

00:16:38.060 --> 00:16:41.220
And then I thought, oh,
right there-- a dog person.

00:16:41.220 --> 00:16:44.420
So we would talk about dogs
and get to know the person

00:16:44.420 --> 00:16:46.520
and get to build
a relationship--

00:16:46.520 --> 00:16:47.840
a personal relationship.

00:16:47.840 --> 00:16:49.682
They would look at the
picture of my niece,

00:16:49.682 --> 00:16:51.140
and they always
say, your daughter?

00:16:51.140 --> 00:16:52.040
I said, no, that's my niece.

00:16:52.040 --> 00:16:53.270
And I'm like, OK, children.

00:16:53.270 --> 00:16:56.540
So children are possibly
an important aspect

00:16:56.540 --> 00:16:58.040
of this individual.

00:16:58.040 --> 00:17:00.080
Beverly Sills-- an opera lover.

00:17:00.080 --> 00:17:03.470
OK, we're going to
talk opera today.

00:17:03.470 --> 00:17:05.540
So find ways.

00:17:05.540 --> 00:17:09.650
This is a practice and it
is an interpersonal practice

00:17:09.650 --> 00:17:12.260
that you should always try.

00:17:12.260 --> 00:17:17.000
And finally, I can't talk
enough about the power

00:17:17.000 --> 00:17:22.400
of grassroots organizing,
even for non-profits.

00:17:22.400 --> 00:17:26.630
Social media and building
a core of volunteers

00:17:26.630 --> 00:17:28.830
are key for anything
that you do.

00:17:28.830 --> 00:17:32.700
And the more you grow, the
more you want these things.

00:17:32.700 --> 00:17:34.490
First of all, because
social media is

00:17:34.490 --> 00:17:37.350
a powerful communication tool.

00:17:37.350 --> 00:17:41.630
It allows you to bring your
message out and information out

00:17:41.630 --> 00:17:45.680
very quickly to many,
many individuals at once

00:17:45.680 --> 00:17:49.850
and people who are your
stakeholders at a very

00:17:49.850 --> 00:17:51.470
low cost--

00:17:51.470 --> 00:17:55.500
sort of a resource.

00:17:55.500 --> 00:18:01.550
So I saw, the other day
I got an email blast--

00:18:01.550 --> 00:18:03.200
a social media blast--

00:18:03.200 --> 00:18:06.230
from a hospital
that we support--

00:18:06.230 --> 00:18:07.790
a local hospital.

00:18:07.790 --> 00:18:13.610
It was a picture of a
crane putting a CAT scan

00:18:13.610 --> 00:18:18.410
machine, a big CAT scan
machine, into the building--

00:18:18.410 --> 00:18:19.880
into the second floor building.

00:18:19.880 --> 00:18:21.740
And it was just a
powerful message.

00:18:21.740 --> 00:18:24.860
And the caption
said, "Thank you.

00:18:24.860 --> 00:18:27.530
Your support made
it possible that we

00:18:27.530 --> 00:18:31.110
were able to purchase this new
equipment for the hospital."

00:18:31.110 --> 00:18:33.540
And it was all done
through social media.

00:18:33.540 --> 00:18:36.810
It had hundreds and hundreds
of people saw it that

00:18:36.810 --> 00:18:38.190
were their
stakeholders, and they

00:18:38.190 --> 00:18:40.960
felt their money had
done something wonderful.

00:18:40.960 --> 00:18:46.710
So, grassroots organizing is
very, very, very important,

00:18:46.710 --> 00:18:49.900
and it's key.

00:18:49.900 --> 00:18:53.470
Now I'm going to spend some time
talking about the "sordid topic

00:18:53.470 --> 00:18:55.780
of coin," as I like to call it.

00:18:55.780 --> 00:18:59.000
Most of you are familiar
with fundraising.

00:18:59.000 --> 00:19:00.250
You've heard the word.

00:19:00.250 --> 00:19:03.010
You know it's the thing
everybody runs away

00:19:03.010 --> 00:19:07.030
from in the non-profit when
they don't know how to do it

00:19:07.030 --> 00:19:10.150
or don't feel
comfortable with it.

00:19:10.150 --> 00:19:12.700
There are traditional methods--
we're going to talk about it.

00:19:12.700 --> 00:19:16.120
And then I'm also going to
talk about capitalization.

00:19:16.120 --> 00:19:18.340
And capitalization
and fundraising

00:19:18.340 --> 00:19:20.650
are two different
things in my book.

00:19:20.650 --> 00:19:24.730
Fundraising-- they are
both two techniques,

00:19:24.730 --> 00:19:28.810
two strategies, for short-term
and long-term sustainability

00:19:28.810 --> 00:19:31.480
of any non-profit.

00:19:31.480 --> 00:19:33.640
It is very important,
I believe today,

00:19:33.640 --> 00:19:36.430
that you have a two-pronged
approach to sustainability.

00:19:36.430 --> 00:19:40.060
One that deals with
the short term and one

00:19:40.060 --> 00:19:41.620
that deals with the long term.

00:19:41.620 --> 00:19:44.380
How am I going to
pay for or have cash

00:19:44.380 --> 00:19:47.440
to pay for my expenses
over the next few months?

00:19:47.440 --> 00:19:52.840
And how am I going to build the
capital over the next few years

00:19:52.840 --> 00:19:55.420
to help me sustain
the organization

00:19:55.420 --> 00:19:56.830
over the next few years?

00:19:56.830 --> 00:19:58.630
So those two things
are important.

00:19:58.630 --> 00:20:00.580
They are both
important, and they

00:20:00.580 --> 00:20:03.880
are part of your institutional
relationship building process.

00:20:03.880 --> 00:20:05.950
This is always ongoing.

00:20:05.950 --> 00:20:08.950
And one does not
capitalize on the other.

00:20:08.950 --> 00:20:11.110
The capitalization
strategy does not

00:20:11.110 --> 00:20:15.110
take money from your regular
fundraising practices.

00:20:15.110 --> 00:20:20.030
So we're going to talk a
little bit about what that is.

00:20:20.030 --> 00:20:22.120
First of all,
traditional fundraising.

00:20:22.120 --> 00:20:25.750
Most of all of us
are familiar with it.

00:20:25.750 --> 00:20:30.530
They are fiscal year to fiscal
year to fiscal year strategies.

00:20:30.530 --> 00:20:35.070
Meaning, if your fiscal year
is January 1st through December

00:20:35.070 --> 00:20:39.040
31st, your traditional
fundraising strategy

00:20:39.040 --> 00:20:42.430
is going to fund
that fiscal year.

00:20:42.430 --> 00:20:45.580
In most non-profits,
especially in the arts,

00:20:45.580 --> 00:20:48.550
there are certain categories
within the traditional

00:20:48.550 --> 00:20:50.200
fundraising tools.

00:20:50.200 --> 00:20:53.080
Number one is individual
giving campaigns.

00:20:53.080 --> 00:20:58.570
That's the email blast
that you get from BSO,

00:20:58.570 --> 00:21:03.220
or Tanglewood, or the Speakeasy
that ask you for money.

00:21:03.220 --> 00:21:05.470
Sometimes it's
phone solicitations.

00:21:05.470 --> 00:21:07.060
Sometimes it's a piece of mail.

00:21:07.060 --> 00:21:12.490
It could be a card
or an actual letter.

00:21:12.490 --> 00:21:16.780
More and more direct
mail is disappearing

00:21:16.780 --> 00:21:21.670
as electronic solicitations
become more efficient and more

00:21:21.670 --> 00:21:24.370
prevalent in the system.

00:21:24.370 --> 00:21:27.010
We have a category
called "major gifts."

00:21:27.010 --> 00:21:31.690
And major gifts is a part
of individual giving.

00:21:31.690 --> 00:21:34.990
But major gift means that
somebody is giving you

00:21:34.990 --> 00:21:37.540
a gift of a
particular amount that

00:21:37.540 --> 00:21:40.720
is significant to
your organization.

00:21:40.720 --> 00:21:42.700
That amount can vary.

00:21:42.700 --> 00:21:47.500
For some people, a $100
donation is significant.

00:21:47.500 --> 00:21:53.110
If most of your donations are
$5, $10, $100 is a big gift.

00:21:53.110 --> 00:21:58.906
If you are an organization
that raises a lot of $100,

00:21:58.906 --> 00:22:07.390
$5,000, $250 gifts, then
a gift of $500 or $1,000

00:22:07.390 --> 00:22:09.160
becomes a major gift.

00:22:09.160 --> 00:22:12.160
And that's coming
from an individual.

00:22:12.160 --> 00:22:15.250
The interesting piece
about major gifts,

00:22:15.250 --> 00:22:18.010
and the important part
about major gifts,

00:22:18.010 --> 00:22:24.940
is that they bring more money
with less amount of work.

00:22:24.940 --> 00:22:29.350
It is very important to
have a grassroots approach

00:22:29.350 --> 00:22:34.660
to individual fundraising,
where you have lots of donors.

00:22:34.660 --> 00:22:40.440
But if you want to raise
$100 and you have a hundred

00:22:40.440 --> 00:22:45.700
$1 donors, it is more
difficult to go and solicit

00:22:45.700 --> 00:22:49.370
a hundred people than
solicit two people for $50.

00:22:49.370 --> 00:22:52.460
So major gifts are
very important.

00:22:52.460 --> 00:22:55.210
They also signal
to the organization

00:22:55.210 --> 00:22:59.140
that this person has a
particular strong affinity

00:22:59.140 --> 00:23:03.160
to your mission, so they
are going to be closer.

00:23:03.160 --> 00:23:08.890
And a $500 gift can be turned
into $1,000, $5,000, and so on.

00:23:08.890 --> 00:23:11.650
So major gifts are important.

00:23:11.650 --> 00:23:17.410
They are personal, and sometimes
it can take years to develop.

00:23:17.410 --> 00:23:22.150
Somebody who gives $10
today may turn into a $50,

00:23:22.150 --> 00:23:25.150
or $100, or $1,000 later on.

00:23:25.150 --> 00:23:30.490
It may require some stewardship,
and some conversations,

00:23:30.490 --> 00:23:33.610
and some attention,
but you can build it.

00:23:33.610 --> 00:23:36.700
Another source of
fund raising income

00:23:36.700 --> 00:23:39.310
is foundations and corporations.

00:23:39.310 --> 00:23:42.250
These gifts vary from
region to region.

00:23:42.250 --> 00:23:45.490
Foundations tend to
be mission-driven.

00:23:45.490 --> 00:23:49.630
Corporations sometimes require
marketing opportunities

00:23:49.630 --> 00:23:50.130
for them.

00:23:53.620 --> 00:23:56.830
In most places, they
are not the most--

00:23:56.830 --> 00:23:58.390
the greater yield.

00:23:58.390 --> 00:24:02.440
Most organizations, especially
in the arts in greater Boston

00:24:02.440 --> 00:24:06.820
and New England, are mostly
supported by individual giving

00:24:06.820 --> 00:24:08.710
and major gifts.

00:24:08.710 --> 00:24:12.280
And then we have,
finally, special events

00:24:12.280 --> 00:24:13.330
and affinity groups.

00:24:13.330 --> 00:24:17.020
For example, a gala event.

00:24:17.020 --> 00:24:19.990
These are social
networking opportunities--

00:24:19.990 --> 00:24:22.370
people love a good party.

00:24:22.370 --> 00:24:27.130
And if you can create a special
event that provides people

00:24:27.130 --> 00:24:31.330
with opportunities
for social networking,

00:24:31.330 --> 00:24:33.160
they'll be coming to your event.

00:24:33.160 --> 00:24:36.250
They are usually transactional.

00:24:36.250 --> 00:24:38.380
One time they may
come to your gala,

00:24:38.380 --> 00:24:41.560
but they may not
give to anything else

00:24:41.560 --> 00:24:43.310
that you have in
your organization--

00:24:43.310 --> 00:24:44.830
any other opportunities.

00:24:44.830 --> 00:24:47.560
But that is OK.

00:24:47.560 --> 00:24:50.920
It's OK to have one-time gifts.

00:24:50.920 --> 00:24:52.610
You could have affinity groups.

00:24:52.610 --> 00:24:54.460
And what I mean by
that are, sometimes,

00:24:54.460 --> 00:24:56.320
what they call
membership groups.

00:24:56.320 --> 00:25:01.570
If an opera company or the
BSO has a young professionals

00:25:01.570 --> 00:25:05.500
affinity group and those
are young individuals

00:25:05.500 --> 00:25:09.820
under the age of 30 that
can give $250 a year.

00:25:09.820 --> 00:25:13.060
So they create an affinity
group, and it's a membership.

00:25:13.060 --> 00:25:15.190
It makes you feel like
you're part of something

00:25:15.190 --> 00:25:18.050
and you're supporting an
organization you like,

00:25:18.050 --> 00:25:20.530
or an institution,
and you're enjoying

00:25:20.530 --> 00:25:24.010
having a group of individuals
with you that share

00:25:24.010 --> 00:25:27.010
that affinity.

00:25:27.010 --> 00:25:29.320
Some of these relationships
can be transactional.

00:25:29.320 --> 00:25:30.700
As I said, it could be one time.

00:25:30.700 --> 00:25:33.430
Some of them can be for
short-term relationships.

00:25:33.430 --> 00:25:38.500
People and foundations change
their focus of giving often.

00:25:38.500 --> 00:25:40.390
This year they
decided that they are

00:25:40.390 --> 00:25:42.400
going to give to
organizations like yours,

00:25:42.400 --> 00:25:46.100
and next year they are going
to give to social services.

00:25:46.100 --> 00:25:49.060
So these things
are less certain.

00:25:49.060 --> 00:25:52.090
That's why traditional
fundraising is important,

00:25:52.090 --> 00:25:56.680
and it's at the core
of any institution.

00:25:56.680 --> 00:26:01.000
But they can be less certain,
and they require resources.

00:26:01.000 --> 00:26:04.120
You need to have
people dedicated solely

00:26:04.120 --> 00:26:09.250
to doing this activity
of raising money.

00:26:09.250 --> 00:26:12.460
The CEO, and the
executive director,

00:26:12.460 --> 00:26:14.470
and the founder of
any organization

00:26:14.470 --> 00:26:20.500
has to be intrinsically involved
in this fundraising effort.

00:26:20.500 --> 00:26:25.120
it could be 50% of his
time, 60%, 75% of his time,

00:26:25.120 --> 00:26:29.530
25% of his time-- it depends
on how good the fundraising

00:26:29.530 --> 00:26:31.480
machine is in place.

00:26:31.480 --> 00:26:34.070
So getting people who
know how to do this,

00:26:34.070 --> 00:26:36.040
it's very important.

00:26:36.040 --> 00:26:40.540
Now I'm going to talk about
capitalization campaigns.

00:26:40.540 --> 00:26:42.100
These are more certain.

00:26:42.100 --> 00:26:49.870
And the reason is that
this inflection of cash

00:26:49.870 --> 00:26:53.170
is in addition to
what you do normally

00:26:53.170 --> 00:26:59.230
on a traditional fiscal
year fundraising campaign.

00:26:59.230 --> 00:27:04.990
So this does not substitute
the other-- this is on top of.

00:27:04.990 --> 00:27:11.980
These are campaigns that
can be 2 to 3 year-- they

00:27:11.980 --> 00:27:14.320
are multi-year.

00:27:14.320 --> 00:27:16.630
They are not the same
as a capital campaign.

00:27:16.630 --> 00:27:19.180
When you hear the
word, capital campaign

00:27:19.180 --> 00:27:24.130
is either the company is
building brick and mortars,

00:27:24.130 --> 00:27:29.680
either a building, or they
are building an endowment,

00:27:29.680 --> 00:27:33.100
and it's a capital
campaign for that purpose.

00:27:33.100 --> 00:27:38.500
Capitalization campaigns are
to enhance your institution

00:27:38.500 --> 00:27:40.060
in the long term.

00:27:40.060 --> 00:27:45.220
It's to provide security
and long-term institutional

00:27:45.220 --> 00:27:47.050
sustainability.

00:27:47.050 --> 00:27:50.630
They promote stabilization
because they are multi-year.

00:27:50.630 --> 00:27:54.130
So you have an
influx of cash coming

00:27:54.130 --> 00:27:57.740
over a period of 2,
3, 4, or 5 years,

00:27:57.740 --> 00:27:59.530
depending on the campaign.

00:27:59.530 --> 00:28:03.800
They are often
contracted commitments.

00:28:03.800 --> 00:28:07.030
If you do a campaign
and you have

00:28:07.030 --> 00:28:10.630
people who are going to give
you a particular number--

00:28:10.630 --> 00:28:13.970
a particular amount of
money over 5 years--

00:28:13.970 --> 00:28:17.240
then you draw a
contract that says,

00:28:17.240 --> 00:28:22.100
"I have pledged to this
organization or to this company

00:28:22.100 --> 00:28:23.300
my support.

00:28:23.300 --> 00:28:27.650
Over the next 5 years, I'm
going to give $5,000 every year

00:28:27.650 --> 00:28:28.950
for the next 5 years."

00:28:28.950 --> 00:28:32.030
So you have $25,000
already committed

00:28:32.030 --> 00:28:34.530
that is going to come
on a yearly basis.

00:28:34.530 --> 00:28:37.700
So when you look
at the future, you

00:28:37.700 --> 00:28:40.010
know that you have
some commitments

00:28:40.010 --> 00:28:44.060
and some pledges on top of
your regular fiscal year

00:28:44.060 --> 00:28:46.130
traditional
fundraising campaign.

00:28:46.130 --> 00:28:48.800
You have an influx
of cash that is

00:28:48.800 --> 00:28:52.970
going to make you more
stable as the years come by.

00:28:52.970 --> 00:28:55.490
We're going to talk about
how you do that-- how

00:28:55.490 --> 00:28:58.820
you make the case for that.

00:28:58.820 --> 00:29:04.260
It offers the organization
the opportunity to innovate,

00:29:04.260 --> 00:29:09.170
to create programs that you
cannot do with your traditional

00:29:09.170 --> 00:29:16.220
fundraising, to produce special
projects that are one-of-a-kind

00:29:16.220 --> 00:29:18.560
that are not going to
be produced every year--

00:29:18.560 --> 00:29:22.610
but maybe you want in 2 years to
do a particular project and you

00:29:22.610 --> 00:29:27.020
need to build the capital
now for that year.

00:29:27.020 --> 00:29:29.840
It engages
stakeholders and donors

00:29:29.840 --> 00:29:33.650
into really long-term
relationships, and this is key.

00:29:33.650 --> 00:29:37.970
You start to learn
who in your network--

00:29:37.970 --> 00:29:41.060
in your stakeholders network--

00:29:41.060 --> 00:29:44.190
is with you for the long haul.

00:29:44.190 --> 00:29:47.820
It's really, really good.

00:29:47.820 --> 00:29:53.120
The other thing is
that donors love

00:29:53.120 --> 00:29:58.280
to give money to organizations
they feel are going to make it.

00:29:58.280 --> 00:30:05.630
If you are building your
future over a 3 or 5-year plan

00:30:05.630 --> 00:30:08.000
and you're asking
for capitalization,

00:30:08.000 --> 00:30:10.730
they feel like,
oh, this person has

00:30:10.730 --> 00:30:16.600
long-term plans and a plan on
how to do that over the next 5

00:30:16.600 --> 00:30:17.100
years.

00:30:17.100 --> 00:30:18.750
I want to be part of that.

00:30:18.750 --> 00:30:23.300
So capitalization campaigns
are, for me, are very exciting.

00:30:23.300 --> 00:30:25.790
If you have a good
case statement,

00:30:25.790 --> 00:30:28.880
they give you a lot of
security, and then it

00:30:28.880 --> 00:30:32.510
allows you to plan more
than 1 year at a time.

00:30:32.510 --> 00:30:37.940
It allows you to build an
overarching 3 to 5-year plan

00:30:37.940 --> 00:30:43.400
with some real
capital behind it.

00:30:43.400 --> 00:30:47.170
I'm going to give you an example
of a capitalization strategy

00:30:47.170 --> 00:30:50.410
that I helped build for Boston
Baroque a few years ago.

00:30:50.410 --> 00:30:52.810
This was about 4 years ago.

00:30:52.810 --> 00:30:57.100
Boston Baroque is a almost
50-years old organization

00:30:57.100 --> 00:30:58.480
in Boston.

00:30:58.480 --> 00:31:05.770
They had been always working
on a year to year basis.

00:31:05.770 --> 00:31:08.740
And there were some plans
that the artistic director

00:31:08.740 --> 00:31:11.140
and the executive director
wanted to put forward

00:31:11.140 --> 00:31:16.240
for the future that require
additional funding that they

00:31:16.240 --> 00:31:22.205
didn't have from the traditional
annual fundraising campaign.

00:31:24.820 --> 00:31:27.160
We call this the "Four
Pillars Campaign,"

00:31:27.160 --> 00:31:31.150
and I'm going to show you
what that means in a second.

00:31:31.150 --> 00:31:33.400
We put together
a case statement,

00:31:33.400 --> 00:31:36.600
and a case statement is
key for any campaign--

00:31:36.600 --> 00:31:38.200
any capitalization campaign.

00:31:38.200 --> 00:31:43.270
It has to eloquently and
succinctly and specifically

00:31:43.270 --> 00:31:45.760
define your goals
of what you want

00:31:45.760 --> 00:31:50.090
to do in the campaign, how
you want to use the money,

00:31:50.090 --> 00:31:54.010
and how are you going to build
the capital over the next 5

00:31:54.010 --> 00:31:57.085
years or whatever the
length of the campaign is.

00:32:03.050 --> 00:32:05.540
For this particular
campaign, at Boston Baroque,

00:32:05.540 --> 00:32:09.800
this was a very rough
and initial look

00:32:09.800 --> 00:32:11.720
at what we were trying to do.

00:32:11.720 --> 00:32:14.870
And we call it the
Four Pillars Campaign

00:32:14.870 --> 00:32:18.140
because they sustain the
missions and programs,

00:32:18.140 --> 00:32:19.580
as you can see there.

00:32:19.580 --> 00:32:24.470
Boston Baroque as an institution
is defined and put together

00:32:24.470 --> 00:32:26.450
by its missions and programs.

00:32:26.450 --> 00:32:29.030
And then the pillars
are the things

00:32:29.030 --> 00:32:33.170
that make the mission
and programs possible.

00:32:33.170 --> 00:32:35.120
There were four things
that we identified

00:32:35.120 --> 00:32:38.240
through a strategic
planning process that

00:32:38.240 --> 00:32:42.360
were key to the stakeholders and
the board of this organization.

00:32:42.360 --> 00:32:46.270
Number one-- they had to do
with artistic excellence.

00:32:46.270 --> 00:32:50.200
We wanted to become financially
stable over a long period

00:32:50.200 --> 00:32:53.560
of time, not just
on an annual basis.

00:32:53.560 --> 00:32:56.560
We wanted to enhance our
community and outreach

00:32:56.560 --> 00:32:59.840
and education
programs, and we wanted

00:32:59.840 --> 00:33:02.930
to enhance our
institutional visibility.

00:33:02.930 --> 00:33:06.890
And I will tell you what
the specifics under each one

00:33:06.890 --> 00:33:08.000
of those categories.

00:33:08.000 --> 00:33:10.580
In each column, there's
a number of activities

00:33:10.580 --> 00:33:13.085
that defined those elements.

00:33:15.890 --> 00:33:18.860
We decided that this campaign
was going to be a $2 million

00:33:18.860 --> 00:33:23.630
campaign to be raised
over 5 years. $2 million

00:33:23.630 --> 00:33:26.310
over a 5-year period.

00:33:26.310 --> 00:33:30.080
And what is this
money going to fund?

00:33:30.080 --> 00:33:33.950
Well, on the first pillar, as
you see, artistic investment--

00:33:33.950 --> 00:33:41.070
the artistic excellence part
column of this campaign.

00:33:41.070 --> 00:33:50.030
We wanted to input an extra
$175,000 into our opera

00:33:50.030 --> 00:33:58.310
productions to make them more
technologically interesting--

00:33:58.310 --> 00:34:02.750
enhancing the quality
of the presentations

00:34:02.750 --> 00:34:06.320
at a theater that
was not a proscenium.

00:34:06.320 --> 00:34:10.520
We wanted to increase the
level of our guest soloists.

00:34:10.520 --> 00:34:13.670
We wanted to bring
people who are working

00:34:13.670 --> 00:34:17.150
at the top of their
artistic excellence-- that

00:34:17.150 --> 00:34:23.389
are performing at the top
of America's theaters today.

00:34:23.389 --> 00:34:27.170
We wanted to bring a
Boston Baroque, which is

00:34:27.170 --> 00:34:29.100
a period-instrument orchestra.

00:34:29.100 --> 00:34:32.630
We wanted to bring players
of particular instruments

00:34:32.630 --> 00:34:36.000
that normally we would not
find people here in Boston.

00:34:36.000 --> 00:34:41.420
So over 5 years, we were
looking to make a $600,000

00:34:41.420 --> 00:34:43.639
investment, over
a 5-year period,

00:34:43.639 --> 00:34:46.730
into our artistic product.

00:34:46.730 --> 00:34:49.100
Then financial stability.

00:34:49.100 --> 00:34:54.800
We wanted to put, over 5
years, a $500,000 investment

00:34:54.800 --> 00:34:56.750
to stabilize the organization.

00:34:56.750 --> 00:34:59.160
And how are we going to do that?

00:34:59.160 --> 00:35:04.070
First of all, build capacity
over a 2-year period.

00:35:04.070 --> 00:35:06.110
And when we talk about
building capacity,

00:35:06.110 --> 00:35:11.660
means bringing on board
staff and team members that

00:35:11.660 --> 00:35:13.550
can help us raise money--

00:35:13.550 --> 00:35:15.150
but going forward.

00:35:15.150 --> 00:35:23.030
So we would invest, over
a 2-year period, $150,000

00:35:23.030 --> 00:35:25.880
to bring a couple
of new staff members

00:35:25.880 --> 00:35:28.370
and then those people
will stay and become

00:35:28.370 --> 00:35:34.430
part of the development machine
within the organization.

00:35:34.430 --> 00:35:37.370
And then, we also wanted
to have some cash reserves

00:35:37.370 --> 00:35:40.760
for the organization in case
things like a pandemic happen,

00:35:40.760 --> 00:35:42.290
right?

00:35:42.290 --> 00:35:45.140
You want to have some cash
so that you don't go under.

00:35:45.140 --> 00:35:48.680
So we figure that
over a 5-year period,

00:35:48.680 --> 00:35:54.230
we would raise $350,000
of a cash reserve fund.

00:35:54.230 --> 00:35:57.530
The third one was community
outreach and education.

00:35:57.530 --> 00:36:02.840
We wanted to invest $100,000
into free community concerts.

00:36:02.840 --> 00:36:05.330
We were doing that
already, but we wanted

00:36:05.330 --> 00:36:07.430
to build a residency program.

00:36:07.430 --> 00:36:12.530
We wanted to invest $100,000
into our classics for kids,

00:36:12.530 --> 00:36:18.830
commissions, and concerts, for a
total of $200,000 over 5 years.

00:36:18.830 --> 00:36:22.010
Then, the institutional
disability part

00:36:22.010 --> 00:36:24.240
was very interesting.

00:36:24.240 --> 00:36:27.050
It was a category that
encompassed things

00:36:27.050 --> 00:36:29.570
that we didn't know
what to put them under.

00:36:29.570 --> 00:36:31.760
And we came up with
institutional disability

00:36:31.760 --> 00:36:34.950
because, eventually, that's
what this was going to do.

00:36:34.950 --> 00:36:40.730
Number one-- we wanted to
build three recording projects

00:36:40.730 --> 00:36:43.760
over the next 5 years.

00:36:43.760 --> 00:36:48.620
See the recording studio
recorded productions

00:36:48.620 --> 00:36:54.020
of operas under our label.

00:36:54.020 --> 00:36:57.710
We wanted to invest in something
that we call Boston Baroque

00:36:57.710 --> 00:37:02.930
Radio, which is a 24/7 digital
radio station that we have.

00:37:02.930 --> 00:37:05.180
We wanted to
establish a presence

00:37:05.180 --> 00:37:10.490
in New York City doing
tours and having a concert

00:37:10.490 --> 00:37:12.860
series in New York City yearly.

00:37:12.860 --> 00:37:15.380
And we wanted to
have more run-outs--

00:37:15.380 --> 00:37:17.930
more touring opportunities--

00:37:17.930 --> 00:37:19.760
going to Tanglewood,
Ravinia, and some

00:37:19.760 --> 00:37:21.440
of the summer festivals.

00:37:21.440 --> 00:37:23.720
In all, the
institutional visibility

00:37:23.720 --> 00:37:27.830
was a $700,000
investment over 5 years.

00:37:27.830 --> 00:37:30.480
For the total of that
campaign-- $2 million.

00:37:30.480 --> 00:37:33.470
So the question is,
OK, so you have a plan.

00:37:33.470 --> 00:37:35.120
You know what you
want to do and how

00:37:35.120 --> 00:37:36.340
you want to spend the money.

00:37:36.340 --> 00:37:41.330
So how do you raise the money
on a capitalization campaign?

00:37:41.330 --> 00:37:45.200
So we came up with
an interesting chart.

00:37:45.200 --> 00:37:47.990
We figured, OK this
is a 5-year campaign.

00:37:50.570 --> 00:37:56.180
We are going to raise
this capital over 5 years.

00:37:56.180 --> 00:37:59.720
There in yellow are
the fiscal years.

00:37:59.720 --> 00:38:02.240
In the first column
is, how many donors

00:38:02.240 --> 00:38:05.510
of the different
categories do we have?

00:38:05.510 --> 00:38:11.060
And we looked at our
donor pool to see

00:38:11.060 --> 00:38:14.870
we could identify some prospects
and have our stakeholders help

00:38:14.870 --> 00:38:18.120
us identify prospects and
then made a commitment.

00:38:18.120 --> 00:38:20.300
These are the number
of people that we

00:38:20.300 --> 00:38:23.510
think we can get on the first
year, on the second year,

00:38:23.510 --> 00:38:24.440
on the third year.

00:38:24.440 --> 00:38:29.510
And what you see happens is
that over a period of 5 years,

00:38:29.510 --> 00:38:32.810
you bring all that
$2 million in.

00:38:32.810 --> 00:38:34.820
On the first year
of the campaign,

00:38:34.820 --> 00:38:38.390
we were able to bring $288,000.

00:38:38.390 --> 00:38:43.560
On the second year, another
$385, and so on, and so on.

00:38:43.560 --> 00:38:46.400
So what you're doing is
you're infusing capital

00:38:46.400 --> 00:38:49.700
to your organization,
over a 5-year period,

00:38:49.700 --> 00:38:53.610
above and separate from
your regular fundraising.

00:38:53.610 --> 00:38:55.340
This is not easy.

00:38:55.340 --> 00:38:58.400
It involves
everybody's commitment.

00:38:58.400 --> 00:39:02.390
Most campaigns, capitalization
campaigns or capital campaigns

00:39:02.390 --> 00:39:04.070
are done--

00:39:04.070 --> 00:39:07.760
they undergo a quiet phase
for the first few years

00:39:07.760 --> 00:39:11.960
until you have 50% of the
capital race of the commitments

00:39:11.960 --> 00:39:13.280
race.

00:39:13.280 --> 00:39:17.660
Once you have 50% of your
pledges, you go public.

00:39:17.660 --> 00:39:20.030
And then people get very
excited and they want

00:39:20.030 --> 00:39:21.980
to get you to the finish line.

00:39:21.980 --> 00:39:25.760
But it involves extra
work and commitment, but

00:39:25.760 --> 00:39:28.040
what you end up learning is--

00:39:28.040 --> 00:39:31.130
who are the folks who have
interests in your organization

00:39:31.130 --> 00:39:36.050
who want to become big
stakeholders with you

00:39:36.050 --> 00:39:41.380
and want to give you a
sustainable plan over 5 years?

00:39:41.380 --> 00:39:44.320
What are people's interests?

00:39:44.320 --> 00:39:46.090
And these plans can change.

00:39:46.090 --> 00:39:49.780
If in the middle of this
you encounter a pandemic,

00:39:49.780 --> 00:39:52.450
you have to look at your
plan and make changes.

00:39:52.450 --> 00:39:55.870
These are living plans.

00:39:55.870 --> 00:39:59.680
So you're always
adapting and changing.

00:39:59.680 --> 00:40:02.140
But you have a
purpose, and that is

00:40:02.140 --> 00:40:06.100
to capitalize your organization
for whatever the reasons

00:40:06.100 --> 00:40:09.980
that you want to do that for.

00:40:09.980 --> 00:40:12.590
And I think that I'm
going to stop there,

00:40:12.590 --> 00:40:15.200
and I am going to
answer questions.

00:40:15.200 --> 00:40:18.120
And I know I've thrown
a lot of information.

00:40:18.120 --> 00:40:19.730
Some of it may make sense.

00:40:19.730 --> 00:40:20.790
Some of it doesn't.

00:40:20.790 --> 00:40:22.730
So, I'm going to open it up.

00:40:22.730 --> 00:40:25.310
And, Shannon Rose, you
can help with that.

00:40:25.310 --> 00:40:26.030
Absolutely.

00:40:26.030 --> 00:40:27.590
So I'm switching
us back to gallery

00:40:27.590 --> 00:40:32.690
view so that we can have a
more peer-to-peer conversation.

00:40:32.690 --> 00:40:35.340
This was fantastic.

00:40:35.340 --> 00:40:37.910
So I think, before we get
into any of the questions

00:40:37.910 --> 00:40:40.070
that I might have had,
why don't we open it up?

00:40:40.070 --> 00:40:42.810
It looks like Evan
has a question.

00:40:42.810 --> 00:40:43.430
Fantastic.

00:40:43.430 --> 00:40:44.990
Thank you so much.

00:40:44.990 --> 00:40:49.640
So I do have a question
on the last slide you had.

00:40:49.640 --> 00:40:55.730
But before getting to that,
I'm curious as to just taxes

00:40:55.730 --> 00:40:59.210
and different things
that 501(c)(3)s qualify

00:40:59.210 --> 00:41:04.160
for, and interesting things that
we can do for our multi-million

00:41:04.160 --> 00:41:09.920
dollar folks who are looking
to tax write-offs for $500,000

00:41:09.920 --> 00:41:12.190
or whatever it may be.

00:41:12.190 --> 00:41:13.620
So your question is about taxes.

00:41:13.620 --> 00:41:17.880
So, non-profits are tax-exempt.

00:41:17.880 --> 00:41:20.970
Anybody that gives
you a gift is tax

00:41:20.970 --> 00:41:23.560
deductible to the
extent of the law.

00:41:23.560 --> 00:41:27.840
I know that the laws have
gotten a little bit tougher

00:41:27.840 --> 00:41:32.040
in the last administration, in
terms of the amount of money

00:41:32.040 --> 00:41:34.740
that you have to give before
it becomes a write-off,

00:41:34.740 --> 00:41:42.980
because the individual deduction
is higher than most people's

00:41:42.980 --> 00:41:46.430
contributions to non-profits.

00:41:46.430 --> 00:41:49.580
But they are all--

00:41:49.580 --> 00:41:58.190
everything that you get into
your capital is tax deductible.

00:41:58.190 --> 00:42:01.640
The only things that you
may pay some taxes on

00:42:01.640 --> 00:42:05.120
are products such
as-- if you sell CDS,

00:42:05.120 --> 00:42:07.100
there is some sort
of taxation to that.

00:42:07.100 --> 00:42:09.560
It's minimum.

00:42:09.560 --> 00:42:14.270
But other than that, any gift
is going to be tax deductible.

00:42:14.270 --> 00:42:15.570
Is that the question?

00:42:15.570 --> 00:42:16.070
Yeah.

00:42:16.070 --> 00:42:18.690
I guess it's going
more into the nuances.

00:42:18.690 --> 00:42:21.590
So I understand 501(c)(3)s
are tax-deductible,

00:42:21.590 --> 00:42:27.110
but I'm curious as to the
specific brackets that might be

00:42:27.110 --> 00:42:30.710
compelling to people.

00:42:30.710 --> 00:42:34.850
It makes no difference
for me to request

00:42:34.850 --> 00:42:37.370
$750,000 versus $1 million.

00:42:37.370 --> 00:42:41.390
But maybe there's
some break at $882,000

00:42:41.390 --> 00:42:44.120
that it actually
incentivizes that person

00:42:44.120 --> 00:42:48.020
to give a larger donation
when it's on the bubble.

00:42:48.020 --> 00:42:51.590
So let me just tell
you how donors think.

00:42:51.590 --> 00:42:56.000
Donors don't look at the
bracket that they want to give.

00:42:56.000 --> 00:43:01.280
They look at, how much
do I have to offer?

00:43:01.280 --> 00:43:03.890
Most donors and
people of wealth who

00:43:03.890 --> 00:43:11.030
have a philanthropic tradition,
they get from their investment

00:43:11.030 --> 00:43:14.957
portfolio folks,
managers, and they say,

00:43:14.957 --> 00:43:16.040
I'm feeling philanthropic.

00:43:16.040 --> 00:43:17.040
I want to continue that.

00:43:17.040 --> 00:43:20.390
You tell me how much
I can give every year.

00:43:20.390 --> 00:43:23.840
The key thing-- if you have
a good development machine

00:43:23.840 --> 00:43:26.390
in place and people
who are knowledgeable

00:43:26.390 --> 00:43:30.410
about major gifts, they will
do research and prospect

00:43:30.410 --> 00:43:32.120
each potential donor.

00:43:32.120 --> 00:43:36.140
So the way it works is that
if you have a person, if it

00:43:36.140 --> 00:43:39.260
is an individual, that you
are going to do a major ask--

00:43:39.260 --> 00:43:43.820
let's say, do I ask for
$75,000 or do I go for $150?

00:43:43.820 --> 00:43:45.860
You need to prospect
them, and there

00:43:45.860 --> 00:43:48.530
are tools available to
development folks where

00:43:48.530 --> 00:43:52.490
they can look at the giving
history of each individual.

00:43:52.490 --> 00:43:55.220
Because every gift that
is given in the non-profit

00:43:55.220 --> 00:43:57.320
is registered
somewhere out there.

00:43:57.320 --> 00:44:03.590
So they can pull that data and
say, this donor's biggest gift

00:44:03.590 --> 00:44:06.070
is $50,000.

00:44:06.070 --> 00:44:09.790
So you don't want to overshoot
because it will turn them off.

00:44:09.790 --> 00:44:13.150
Because then they'll think
that if you ask for $150,

00:44:13.150 --> 00:44:16.750
and they normally don't give
more than $50,000, they may go,

00:44:16.750 --> 00:44:19.310
oh, they're looking for
a bigger donor than I am.

00:44:19.310 --> 00:44:20.892
I'm going to pass.

00:44:20.892 --> 00:44:22.100
So you don't want to do that.

00:44:22.100 --> 00:44:25.600
So it's not how they
fall in one bracket.

00:44:25.600 --> 00:44:29.980
It's more, what is their
giving history and tradition?

00:44:29.980 --> 00:44:34.210
And you need to do your homework
as part of your development

00:44:34.210 --> 00:44:38.170
work on learning
about that donor.

00:44:38.170 --> 00:44:39.520
What does he give to?

00:44:39.520 --> 00:44:41.890
How much?

00:44:41.890 --> 00:44:45.550
And then the tools will tell
you the most likely gift

00:44:45.550 --> 00:44:48.460
that you can ask, and then
you ask for a little bit more.

00:44:48.460 --> 00:44:51.490
If they normally give
$50, I would ask for $75.

00:44:51.490 --> 00:44:54.870
And hopefully they'll
land somewhere in between.

00:44:54.870 --> 00:44:55.410
Does that--

00:44:55.410 --> 00:44:57.750
And then, in terms
of approaching

00:44:57.750 --> 00:45:00.918
a foundation versus a
high-net-worth individual,

00:45:00.918 --> 00:45:02.460
what are some
considerations that you

00:45:02.460 --> 00:45:04.140
have from a tax standpoint?

00:45:04.140 --> 00:45:05.010
The same way.

00:45:05.010 --> 00:45:08.260
There are no tax implications
for a foundation.

00:45:08.260 --> 00:45:11.490
They are also
non-profits themselves

00:45:11.490 --> 00:45:12.750
when they're established.

00:45:12.750 --> 00:45:16.560
It is all about their history
giving; how much do they have?

00:45:16.560 --> 00:45:18.570
The foundations normally work.

00:45:18.570 --> 00:45:19.830
We have a pool of money.

00:45:19.830 --> 00:45:21.960
We have a million dollars
to give this year.

00:45:21.960 --> 00:45:24.150
And how we're going
to divvy the pie.

00:45:24.150 --> 00:45:29.850
Most foundations have an annual
report that will tell you,

00:45:29.850 --> 00:45:36.660
in a pie chart, how much
they give to universities

00:45:36.660 --> 00:45:38.880
and education, how much
they give to hospitals,

00:45:38.880 --> 00:45:42.150
social services, and arts
and cultural organizations,

00:45:42.150 --> 00:45:46.530
and then what your
slice of the pie is.

00:45:46.530 --> 00:45:50.330
So that's how you deal with it.

00:45:50.330 --> 00:45:52.740
It's exactly the same.

00:45:52.740 --> 00:45:56.670
And most times, most
foundations guidelines

00:45:56.670 --> 00:46:00.110
will tell you
their maximum gift.

00:46:00.110 --> 00:46:00.800
Great.

00:46:00.800 --> 00:46:02.920
Thanks.

00:46:02.920 --> 00:46:05.560
So then you ask for
the maximum, right?

00:46:05.560 --> 00:46:06.490
Absolutely.

00:46:06.490 --> 00:46:09.450
Every time.

00:46:09.450 --> 00:46:11.890
We have few questions
that Shannon Rose and I

00:46:11.890 --> 00:46:12.640
have talked about.

00:46:12.640 --> 00:46:14.057
Would you have any
other questions

00:46:14.057 --> 00:46:17.410
from people who are attending
and not a workshop host?

00:46:21.580 --> 00:46:22.430
OK.

00:46:22.430 --> 00:46:23.110
Well I will--

00:46:23.110 --> 00:46:24.020
I have a question.

00:46:24.020 --> 00:46:24.340
Can you hear me?

00:46:24.340 --> 00:46:25.000
[INAUDIBLE]

00:46:25.000 --> 00:46:26.830
Yeah, go ahead.

00:46:26.830 --> 00:46:27.970
Yeah.

00:46:27.970 --> 00:46:29.050
My name is Joseph Webbie.

00:46:29.050 --> 00:46:33.880
By way of background, my startup
won one of the MIT hackathons

00:46:33.880 --> 00:46:36.580
in June in the
Knowledge Economy track.

00:46:36.580 --> 00:46:40.330
And we're building a marketplace
to provide AI products

00:46:40.330 --> 00:46:41.980
and services--

00:46:41.980 --> 00:46:44.530
sort of productizing
it and removing

00:46:44.530 --> 00:46:46.570
the friction for companies.

00:46:46.570 --> 00:46:49.360
And over the last few
years, I did some work

00:46:49.360 --> 00:46:52.510
for the Harvard
Business School alumni--

00:46:52.510 --> 00:46:54.940
the community Partners Program--
where we volunteer and do

00:46:54.940 --> 00:46:58.090
non-profit consulting for
non-profits in New York

00:46:58.090 --> 00:47:00.460
City and the Bay Area.

00:47:00.460 --> 00:47:02.980
And one thing I found
that's really common

00:47:02.980 --> 00:47:05.980
is the difficulty
in these non-profits

00:47:05.980 --> 00:47:09.320
with visualizing the
impact of their work

00:47:09.320 --> 00:47:11.600
so that they can
raise more money.

00:47:11.600 --> 00:47:13.635
Number two-- they don't
have a data strategy.

00:47:13.635 --> 00:47:15.010
And number three--
they're really

00:47:15.010 --> 00:47:17.980
bad with everything digital
marketing and social media.

00:47:17.980 --> 00:47:19.570
So my question,
to you and Miguel,

00:47:19.570 --> 00:47:22.600
is we're working
on a product where

00:47:22.600 --> 00:47:29.200
we can allow non-profits to have
access to a data science team.

00:47:29.200 --> 00:47:32.810
Sort of let's say on a
monthly subscription basis.

00:47:32.810 --> 00:47:35.950
So let's say for $500, you
have one data scientist that

00:47:35.950 --> 00:47:38.860
would work on a
visualizing all your work.

00:47:38.860 --> 00:47:41.470
So I just wanted to get
your feedback on that idea.

00:47:41.470 --> 00:47:42.050
I think--

00:47:42.050 --> 00:47:42.410
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:47:42.410 --> 00:47:44.290
--could connect with
you [? afterwards. ?]

00:47:44.290 --> 00:47:50.020
I think the reason that you see
that non-profits are based--

00:47:50.020 --> 00:47:52.180
and they work out
a mission ambition.

00:47:52.180 --> 00:47:54.430
They have a mission.

00:47:54.430 --> 00:47:59.410
One of the most common
issues that we see

00:47:59.410 --> 00:48:06.640
is that they are program people.

00:48:06.640 --> 00:48:12.040
If the mission is an
orchestra, they are musicians.

00:48:12.040 --> 00:48:14.000
They don't know the
business side of it.

00:48:14.000 --> 00:48:15.625
They don't know the tools.

00:48:15.625 --> 00:48:19.450
They are not data
analytical people.

00:48:19.450 --> 00:48:25.510
If it is a social services--
if it is feeding the hunger,

00:48:25.510 --> 00:48:28.600
then they are program.

00:48:28.600 --> 00:48:33.040
They don't know the
importance of data analysis

00:48:33.040 --> 00:48:34.070
and all of that.

00:48:34.070 --> 00:48:34.600
So, yeah.

00:48:34.600 --> 00:48:37.780
So that's why you saw
that in your experience.

00:48:37.780 --> 00:48:39.970
And these are really
great dedicated people

00:48:39.970 --> 00:48:41.950
with great missions,
but they get

00:48:41.950 --> 00:48:46.180
stuck in a cycle that doesn't
allow them to grow unless they

00:48:46.180 --> 00:48:49.750
bring on board people with the
technology, with the knowledge,

00:48:49.750 --> 00:48:53.740
and the skill to do
what you're offering.

00:48:53.740 --> 00:48:55.240
I think it's a great idea.

00:48:55.240 --> 00:48:58.690
If you can do
that, it'll be very

00:48:58.690 --> 00:49:02.830
interesting to see
how difficult it

00:49:02.830 --> 00:49:05.440
is for you to analyze
qualitative data

00:49:05.440 --> 00:49:08.560
versus quantitative data.

00:49:08.560 --> 00:49:14.020
It's easier to say how
many people we feed.

00:49:14.020 --> 00:49:19.090
It's much harder to say how many
people we bring out of poverty

00:49:19.090 --> 00:49:22.960
because that's a
different analysis.

00:49:22.960 --> 00:49:24.560
But I think it's phenomenal.

00:49:24.560 --> 00:49:26.050
I mean, what a great idea.

00:49:26.050 --> 00:49:30.340
And yes, most small
mid-level non-profits

00:49:30.340 --> 00:49:33.280
don't have the
skill sets for that.

00:49:33.280 --> 00:49:33.780
Thank you.

00:49:33.780 --> 00:49:36.000
I like the persona that you
mentioned of the analyzers.

00:49:36.000 --> 00:49:37.810
So hopefully, if you can
please share your email,

00:49:37.810 --> 00:49:40.477
I'd love to connect with you and
I'll share my product with you.

00:49:40.477 --> 00:49:42.090
Thank you so much.

00:49:42.090 --> 00:49:45.420
So piggybacking off of one
of those, I've got one.

00:49:45.420 --> 00:49:47.670
Having a long and
storied history

00:49:47.670 --> 00:49:51.420
in under-resourced
non-profits myself,

00:49:51.420 --> 00:49:54.300
there's a baggage
element that surrounds

00:49:54.300 --> 00:49:58.050
this lean, non-profit
mentality, and it negatively

00:49:58.050 --> 00:50:00.700
impacts arts-driven
startup organizations.

00:50:00.700 --> 00:50:04.080
So how do you recommend
planning, thinking,

00:50:04.080 --> 00:50:08.880
budgeting for sustainability
to avoid the burnout

00:50:08.880 --> 00:50:11.520
and other difficulties
that are all too prevalent

00:50:11.520 --> 00:50:14.940
and the shortcomings that
were just brought up?

00:50:14.940 --> 00:50:17.430
You need to think big.

00:50:17.430 --> 00:50:20.770
If you are going to venture
into non-profit, think big.

00:50:20.770 --> 00:50:22.710
What is the overarching goal?

00:50:22.710 --> 00:50:23.940
How do you get there?

00:50:23.940 --> 00:50:28.570
And then bring people on board
that can help you with it.

00:50:28.570 --> 00:50:32.370
Most people who are founding
members or founding directors

00:50:32.370 --> 00:50:35.130
of an organization, they
are, again, program folks.

00:50:35.130 --> 00:50:39.750
They know how to do the product
that they want to create.

00:50:39.750 --> 00:50:43.020
They just don't know
how to capitalize it.

00:50:43.020 --> 00:50:44.970
So, it is hard.

00:50:44.970 --> 00:50:48.480
You need to be entrepreneurial.

00:50:48.480 --> 00:50:51.600
It doesn't matter if you're in
the arts, or social services,

00:50:51.600 --> 00:50:52.320
or hospital.

00:50:52.320 --> 00:50:54.000
You need to be entrepreneurial.

00:50:54.000 --> 00:50:57.480
And come up with a business
plan that gives you

00:50:57.480 --> 00:50:59.940
the capital that you need.

00:50:59.940 --> 00:51:01.530
Don't under-fund.

00:51:01.530 --> 00:51:02.760
Don't be lean.

00:51:02.760 --> 00:51:06.915
You make your case to
every donor that you meet.

00:51:06.915 --> 00:51:09.270
You know, I cannot
do it with $50.

00:51:09.270 --> 00:51:11.610
I need $100.

00:51:11.610 --> 00:51:16.200
$50 will not allow me to
survive going forward,

00:51:16.200 --> 00:51:17.440
and this is very important.

00:51:17.440 --> 00:51:21.120
You need to be very
compelling in your message

00:51:21.120 --> 00:51:23.850
to every single person.

00:51:23.850 --> 00:51:26.790
Then the other piece
is board members.

00:51:26.790 --> 00:51:29.430
You need to build
a board of people

00:51:29.430 --> 00:51:32.280
that not only believe
in your mission

00:51:32.280 --> 00:51:34.320
but who can help you fund it--

00:51:34.320 --> 00:51:36.960
who are connected.

00:51:36.960 --> 00:51:38.550
And you need to be very savvy.

00:51:38.550 --> 00:51:40.230
It's going to be a
mixture of people

00:51:40.230 --> 00:51:44.880
who have capacity to give
you a big check and people

00:51:44.880 --> 00:51:50.220
who don't have capacity but who
can make connections for you.

00:51:50.220 --> 00:51:52.200
Learn the skills
that you don't know.

00:51:52.200 --> 00:51:54.690
If you are starting a
non-profit and you don't know

00:51:54.690 --> 00:51:56.760
how to ask for money, learn it.

00:51:56.760 --> 00:52:01.390
It's not rocket science.

00:52:01.390 --> 00:52:04.260
It's an art.

00:52:04.260 --> 00:52:07.330
Ask for advice, get money.

00:52:07.330 --> 00:52:09.690
Ask for money, get advice.

00:52:09.690 --> 00:52:13.650
So when you don't
know a person--

00:52:13.650 --> 00:52:14.880
ask for advice.

00:52:14.880 --> 00:52:17.010
Call him up-- can I
have coffee with you?

00:52:17.010 --> 00:52:20.070
I have a project and I
would love your advice.

00:52:20.070 --> 00:52:22.980
And see if they get
interested in your project

00:52:22.980 --> 00:52:24.630
and then get them on board.

00:52:24.630 --> 00:52:26.950
Stewardship takes time.

00:52:26.950 --> 00:52:31.080
It takes time but it has
to happen constantly.

00:52:31.080 --> 00:52:33.670
And don't do things on your own.

00:52:33.670 --> 00:52:36.420
Don't ever be alone.

00:52:36.420 --> 00:52:39.660
Get a lot of people around
you that share your passion,

00:52:39.660 --> 00:52:41.400
and then you can do it.

00:52:41.400 --> 00:52:44.970
But don't under budget.

00:52:44.970 --> 00:52:46.410
Don't settle.

00:52:46.410 --> 00:52:48.420
Just be tenacious.

00:52:48.420 --> 00:52:52.200
There is a good book call
The Ultimate Sales Machine,

00:52:52.200 --> 00:52:56.430
and it talks about
pigheaded persistency.

00:52:56.430 --> 00:53:00.605
I think it's key
in non-profit work.

00:53:00.605 --> 00:53:01.980
That's really
great advice, and I

00:53:01.980 --> 00:53:05.130
think that is definitely
applicable to all

00:53:05.130 --> 00:53:07.530
different types of ventures
and all the different kind

00:53:07.530 --> 00:53:10.530
of students who will be
competing in this competition.

00:53:10.530 --> 00:53:15.240
And speaking of, if you have one
takeaway or one pearl of wisdom

00:53:15.240 --> 00:53:16.860
that you would give
to students who

00:53:16.860 --> 00:53:20.370
are looking to enter
a arts-related venture

00:53:20.370 --> 00:53:23.720
in this competition,
what would that be?

00:53:23.720 --> 00:53:27.770
I would say work from purpose.

00:53:27.770 --> 00:53:30.070
What is your purpose?

00:53:30.070 --> 00:53:33.530
I discovered mid
career when I was

00:53:33.530 --> 00:53:37.070
debating whether to go
into political advocacy

00:53:37.070 --> 00:53:42.620
or fundraising in
a more bigger way.

00:53:42.620 --> 00:53:46.430
I went through a whole
executive training program.

00:53:46.430 --> 00:53:49.220
In over the year
period of this program,

00:53:49.220 --> 00:53:52.970
I learned what my purpose
was, which was the arts.

00:53:52.970 --> 00:53:57.050
And it was music, and it
was working with artists.

00:53:57.050 --> 00:54:01.010
And when I decided, yeah,
that's my purpose, then worked

00:54:01.010 --> 00:54:04.260
from it, everything
fell into place.

00:54:04.260 --> 00:54:05.540
I was very excited.

00:54:05.540 --> 00:54:10.010
The interesting part is when I
talk to folks about what I do

00:54:10.010 --> 00:54:13.850
or a product that I'm selling,
what they say is, wow,

00:54:13.850 --> 00:54:15.080
you're so excited about it.

00:54:15.080 --> 00:54:17.390
And I never think about
it, but I guess I am.

00:54:17.390 --> 00:54:19.490
Because I'm working on purpose.

00:54:19.490 --> 00:54:22.130
So find your purpose.

00:54:22.130 --> 00:54:24.140
Work on purpose.

00:54:24.140 --> 00:54:25.910
That's the number the one thing.

00:54:25.910 --> 00:54:29.830
Everything else
will fall in place.

00:54:29.830 --> 00:54:32.020
I couldn't have asked
for a better conclusion.

00:54:32.020 --> 00:54:33.670
We're coming to the end.

00:54:33.670 --> 00:54:37.790
If anybody else has any final
wrap-up questions for Miguel,

00:54:37.790 --> 00:54:38.290
go.

00:54:38.290 --> 00:54:39.040
Speak now.

00:54:39.040 --> 00:54:42.340
Make yourself known.

00:54:42.340 --> 00:54:43.642
Going.

00:54:43.642 --> 00:54:45.730
Going.

